WEBVTT

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:09.818
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Mountain Rescue Team volunteers like Gordon and David provide

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:15.064
an extremely valuable service for Lake District walkers.

00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:18.174
Meet Stephanie Coulshed.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.680
Last year, Stephanie decided to go walking in the Lake District,

00:00:23.111 --> 00:00:27.520
but her weekend away in the great outdoors didn’t quite go according to plan.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:34.320
<v><b>Stephanie:</b></v> So, I’m Stephanie. Um, I work for, um, a disability charity in London.

00:00:34.642 --> 00:00:39.800
Um, I’ve always loved the Lake District and walking in the Lakes and I’ve got friends who feel the same.

00:00:39.815 --> 00:00:45.852
So, back in October, um, I was up in the Lake District for a weekend with a group of friends.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:51.835
We walked, um, from Borrowdale, we walked, um, up onto the, um,

00:00:51.958 --> 00:00:54.560
eastern end of the High Spy Ridge.

00:00:55.874 --> 00:01:02.840
And then, on the descent, um, we, we were coming down, um, a really steep stretch.

00:01:02.866 --> 00:01:06.040
I’d been really careful over the…the rocky bits.

00:01:06.124 --> 00:01:13.320
And then, um, got down from the rocky bits, and I just hit a bit of bog, a really slippy bit of bog.

00:01:13.644 --> 00:01:18.854
My right foot shot from under me and I fell backwards and sideways, um,

00:01:18.879 --> 00:01:21.560
over my left ankle and heard a crack.

00:01:21.695 --> 00:01:31.320
So, I was left, um, basically, er, stuck in a bog, um, and I had to crawl out on all fours,

00:01:32.455 --> 00:01:36.524
cos I knew that I shouldn’t stand or try to stand.

00:01:36.687 --> 00:01:42.132
<v><b>Gordon:</b></v> Stephanie’s call-out was a typical call-out for us, in that it was a lower leg injury,

00:01:42.157 --> 00:01:47.480
that’s maybe 40%, maybe more, of, of the call-outs that we have.

00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:50.405
<v><b>Stephanie:</b></v> So, I just shouted, ‘Help!’ and, uh, the,

00:01:50.879 --> 00:01:55.378
the person in front of me just turned around and said, ‘Are you OK?’,

00:01:55.403 --> 00:01:59.439
and I just went, ‘No!’ ’cos I really knew by then that I wasn’t.

00:01:59.764 --> 00:02:03.920
<v><b>Gordon:</b></v> It’s one of the trickier routes that we have on our patch.

00:02:03.945 --> 00:02:05.520
It’s quite steep ground,

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:12.406
and there’s a steep-sided narrow valley with a ghyll running down the middle of it, a steep stream.

00:02:12.977 --> 00:02:17.903
<v><b>Stephanie:</b></v> The other complication was that where we were, there was no phone signal at all.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:26.751
Um, so, um, my friend’s daughter is a, is a fell runner, and she’s the fastest runner in the group.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:32.866
So, she ran down to get to a phone, you know, a landline or something down in the valley.

00:02:33.154 --> 00:02:35.799
So, we sent her down to phone for Mountain Rescue.

00:02:36.996 --> 00:02:39.510
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> When the call came, Gordon and the team dropped

00:02:39.522 --> 00:02:41.730
what they were doing and raced to the site.

00:02:42.908 --> 00:02:49.345
<v><b>Stephanie:</b></v> So, um, we waited, um, we waited, it felt like forever.

00:02:49.392 --> 00:02:51.673
It was probably a couple of hours, um, from when

00:02:51.698 --> 00:02:57.362
I fell to when we saw those really welcome red jackets coming up the hill towards us.

00:03:00.098 --> 00:03:02.915
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Gordon was in the second vehicle to arrive on the scene

00:03:03.314 --> 00:03:05.297
and he called up to the advanced team.

00:03:06.455 --> 00:03:08.143
<v><b>Gordon:</b></v> And when we arrived on scene,

00:03:08.168 --> 00:03:12.681
the guys from [the] first truck had done a good job of assessing the nature of the injuries

00:03:12.706 --> 00:03:15.047
and treating them accordingly.

00:03:15.071 --> 00:03:20.570
And they’d already, uh, given her pain relief and splinted her limb.

00:03:21.006 --> 00:03:23.737
So, all we had to do really was help package her.

00:03:23.762 --> 00:03:27.320
So, put the stretcher together, make sure she’s all wrapped up warmly,

00:03:27.338 --> 00:03:31.355
and then attach her to the stretcher so she’s not going to fall out of it.

00:03:32.199 --> 00:03:35.836
<v><b>Stephanie:</b></v> Um, there was like, they strapped me into the stretcher really tightly.

00:03:36.511 --> 00:03:42.457
And then there was a kind of, um, perspex hood thing that came down over my face,

00:03:42.708 --> 00:03:47.026
obviously to protect me from any, you know, if any stones got knocked or anything like that.

00:03:47.388 --> 00:03:51.709
<v><b>Gordon:</b></v> It’s tricky ground at the top, it’s very steep and grassy and quite slippy.

00:03:51.734 --> 00:03:56.502
So, we can drag the sledge along the ground to begin with, which saves us a lot of effort.

00:03:57.617 --> 00:03:59.987
And we did that for the first 50, 100 metres,

00:04:00.012 --> 00:04:04.000
but then we came to a stream and we had to carry her through and over the stream.

00:04:04.539 --> 00:04:07.756
And that’s always a place where you focus the mind.

00:04:08.178 --> 00:04:12.073
You don’t want to drop the stretcher at that point and have her tobogganing down the mountain.

00:04:12.092 --> 00:04:12.859
That wouldn’t look good.

00:04:13.549 --> 00:04:16.739
So, we carried her through that, and then from there on down,

00:04:17.334 --> 00:04:22.000
it’s pretty steep, quite narrow, and it’s stony, so we have to carry the stretcher there.

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:27.806
<v><b>Stephanie:</b></v> Um, and everything they did, it felt like it was really well-rehearsed

00:04:27.831 --> 00:04:33.160
and everyone knew exactly what to say and exactly what to do. And it just all clicked into place.

00:04:33.353 --> 00:04:35.885
And that was one of the things that felt really reassuring.

00:04:36.203 --> 00:04:42.795
<v><b>Gordon:</b></v> And within, sort of, 20, 25 minutes, we were down on the, the bottom of the,

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:49.226
the fell and able to then drag her again, um, over a couple of fields to the stretcher…er,

00:04:49.414 --> 00:04:53.032
sorry, to the ambulance that was waiting, er, by the bridge.

00:04:55.983 --> 00:04:59.914
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Stephanie was taken to hospital, where she had surgery the following day.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:05.461
Now recovered, she’s hugely grateful to the Mountain Rescue Team.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:10.502
<v><b>Stephanie:</b></v> Well, I guess, if I was to, if I was to have them sitting in front of me now,

00:05:10.527 --> 00:05:13.160
I mean, obviously, I’d say like a massive thank you.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:18.335
But I’d also say, you know, that they have…well, for me anyway,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:24.093
they had exactly the right kind of mix of being totally professional,

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:27.061
but also being really down to earth and friendly.

00:05:27.307 --> 00:05:32.183
And the combination of those two things just made me feel really safe.

00:05:32.837 --> 00:05:35.338
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Mountain rescue is a serious business.

00:05:36.752 --> 00:05:41.222
Mountain rescue volunteers train in some of Europe’s most difficult conditions…

00:05:42.549 --> 00:05:45.738
learning to use specialized equipment and technology…

00:05:47.086 --> 00:05:49.771
and even working with trained rescue dogs.

00:05:53.055 --> 00:05:56.720
And they do all this so they’re ready to go when the call comes.

00:06:00.437 --> 00:06:03.963
The magnificent Lake District landscape invites adventure.

00:06:04.448 --> 00:06:09.100
But when you come, as the Mountain Rescue Teams say, ‘be adventure smart’

00:06:09.641 --> 00:06:15.677
– come prepared, check the weather, and only attempt an activity within your capabilities.